{"cluster_id": "C-2614", "topic": "Heartbeat classification uses live, terminated, and expired; an expired", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B002-012", "B002-059", "B004-052", "B004-148", "B005-020", "B016-061"], "canonical_claim": "Heartbeat classification uses `live`, `terminated`, and `expired`; an expired non-terminated mount is fenced with a token-guarded overwrite that preserves its body and sets `gc_fenced`. The mount heartbeat's `min_active` field records the oldest in-flight `build_seq`, while `UINT64_MAX` is the farewell or retired sentinel. GC classifies a mount with `gc_fenced` as already fenced and a mount with `min_active == UINT64_MAX` as cleanly terminated; fencing preserves the body and uses token-guarded `putOverwrite`. An expired heartbeat without a terminated stamp is fenced out with a token-guarded overwrite that sets `gc_fenced` and bumps `seq`; successful fence-out makes the sleeper's next renewal fail and excludes it from the acknowledgement floor. The heartbeat floor excludes terminated mounts using `min_active == UINT64_MAX`, fences expired-unfenced mounts with token-guarded overwrite, and re-reads on a fence-out precondition failure before classifying the entry. A `MountLease` uses `UINT64_MAX` in `min_active` as the retired farewell sentinel, and `gc_fenced` is a terminal GC fence-out flag.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/04-gc-protocol.md#heartbeat-floor", "docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#heartbeat-floor", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-02-cas-gc-ack-floor-fence.md#L655-L694", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-15-cas-codecs-v3-phase2-control-plane.md#task3", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/04-ops-mounts.md#2-2-lease-heartbeat-mechanics", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-02-cas-gc-ack-floor-fence-redesign.md#gc-round-protocol-one-pass"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2615", "topic": "The three server-root control objects live under gc/server-roots/<srid>/: owner", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-009", "AUDIT-M2-188", "B002-056", "B016-060"], "canonical_claim": "The three server-root control objects live under `gc/server-roots/<srid>/`: `owner` is a write-once identity anchor, `epoch` is a CAS-bumped monotone counter, and `mount` is a token-guarded lease body.; The server-root text objects are `cas_owner`, `cas_epoch`, and `cas_mount_lease`: the owner binds a server-root id to a server UUID, the epoch stores the monotone next writer epoch, and the mount lease stores the live holder and merged `min_active` state.; The durable per-server_root_id identity is built from three sticky objects under gc/server-roots/<server_root_id>/: owner (permanent, putIfAbsent once, binds server_root_id to one server_uuid_hex forever), epoch (permanent, CAS-bumped per writable open, a durable monotone writer_epoch counter never reissued), and mount (an expirable heartbeat lease, same-UUID reclaim only after expiry). The identity/fencing model has three durable layers with a fixed startup order: `OwnerProto`/owner is identity (write-once binding of `server_root_id` to `server_uuid`, clock-free, never deleted or reassigned, foreign UUID fails closed even if a lease is expired); `ServerEpochProto`/epoch is the durable monotone fence (CAS-bumped on each mount attempt, hands out a fresh `writer_epoch`, must survive lease expiry/reclaim, and a missing epoch over non-empty data is a reset hazard); `MountLeaseProto`/mount is liveness (renewed repeatedly, has wall-clock expiry/seq/hostname/pid diagnostics, reclaimable when expired or GC-fenced). Startup order is exactly owner then epoch then mount.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/01-architecture.md#incarnation-identity", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-15-cas-codecs-v3-phase2-control-plane.md#task3", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/04-ops-mounts.md#2-1-control-objects-all-under-gc-server-roots-srid", "docs/superpowers/reports/reviews.md#L1444-L1470"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2616", "topic": "server_root_id must be non-empty, at most 255 bytes, contain", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B002-057"], "canonical_claim": "`server_root_id` must be non-empty, at most 255 bytes, contain no empty, `.` or `..` path segment, and not use reserved `_files` or `_manifests` segments; invalid IDs fail with `BAD_ARGUMENTS`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/04-ops-mounts.md#2-1-control-objects-all-under-gc-server-roots-srid"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2617", "topic": "GC fence-out of an expired lease and the Store's", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B002-060"], "canonical_claim": "GC fence-out of an expired lease and the Store's local write-fence are distinct mechanisms: GC persists `gc_fenced=true`, while Store latches local `lost` and gates ordinary ref mutations through `mayMutate()`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/04-ops-mounts.md#2-3-fencing"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2618", "topic": "A GC-fenced (uuid, epoch) mount cannot be refreshed in", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B002-061", "B002-062", "B006-103", "B006-105", "B006-107", "B007-001", "B007-002", "B007-003", "B007-009", "B035-012"], "canonical_claim": "A GC-fenced `(uuid, epoch)` mount cannot be refreshed in place; recovery must allocate a fresh writer epoch.; A same-UUID, same-epoch fenced mount yields `FencedSelf`, a foreign owner yields `ForeignOwner`, and a same-UUID live different epoch yields `LiveDoubleStart` from `claimMount`. `MountClaimResult::FencedSelf` represents a same-UUID, same-epoch mount body with `gc_fenced=true`; `claimMount` must not write a refresh in that case, and a different epoch may reclaim immediately. `MountLeaseKeeper::claim` must refuse adoption of a fenced body when the observed UUID and epoch equal the current incarnation, and the caller must recover as a new incarnation. The same-UUID/same-epoch refresh branch of `claimMount` must also refuse a fenced body; only a different epoch may reclaim it.; `Store::open` retries fence recovery at most three times; on `FencedSelf` or `MountFencedException` it allocates a fresh writer epoch, rebuilds the keeper, and retries, while the final `armMountFence` uses the final epoch. `Store::open` must wrap claim and keeper startup in a bounded retry loop; an adoption refusal classified as `fenced_by_gc` allocates a fresh epoch and retries, while foreign or superseded refusals abort.; A mount body with `gc_fenced = true` is terminal for its `(server_uuid, writer_epoch)` incarnation; recovery must use a fresh `writer_epoch`.; The mount-lease recovery protocol follows the TLA+ rule that a GC fence costs an epoch: a fenced same-epoch body is never adoptable or refreshable, and recovery always allocates a new writer epoch. Mount fence recovery treats a `FencedSelf` claim as a typed `MountFencedException`, distinguishes a GC fence of the node's own expired lease from a foreign writer, allocates a fresh `writer_epoch`, and retries the claim in `Store::open`.", "issue_ids": ["P1", "S13"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-06-cas-mount-lease-fence-recovery.md#L256-L316", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-06-cas-mount-lease-fence-recovery.md#L5-L17", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-06-cas-mount-lease-fence-recovery.md#L75-L113", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/04-ops-mounts.md#2-3-fencing", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/04-ops-mounts.md#2-4-claimmount-decision-matrix-casserverroot-cpp-337", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-06-cas-mount-lease-fence-recovery-design.md#fence-costs-epoch", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-06-cas-mount-lease-fence-recovery-design.md#open-retry", "utils/ca-soak/scenarios/BACKLOG.md#L912-L917"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2619", "topic": "A hard-killed server's stale mount is waited out for", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B002-064", "B005-059"], "canonical_claim": "A hard-killed server's stale mount is waited out for at most the lease TTL plus margin before reclaim, while a live twin causes `LiveDoubleStart` and aborts. The mount-reclaim wait deadline is latched from the first stale lease's `expires_at_ms` plus a margin and capped at no more than one TTL plus margin from the wait start; no new user setting is introduced.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/04-ops-mounts.md#2-6-restart-stale-fenced-behavior", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-02-cas-mount-crash-recovery-design.md#wait-semantics"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2620", "topic": "When a running server detects that GC fenced its", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B002-065"], "canonical_claim": "When a running server detects that GC fenced its mount, it latches the local write fence, schedules remount, and retries with a fresh epoch without throwing from the remount loop.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/04-ops-mounts.md#b-lease-lapse-gc-fences-mount-server-detects-self-remount"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2621", "topic": "The mount model requires sticky server-root ownership, a durable", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B002-168", "B014-066", "B043-026"], "canonical_claim": "The mount model requires sticky server-root ownership, a durable monotone writer epoch, and observation-based reclaim over a stable holder token for a full TTL-plus-drift window on the reclaimer's monotonic clock. Observation-based reclaim waits for a stable mount token for `TTL + Drift` on the reclaimer's clock before advancing the durable epoch; the reclaim installs the successor's fresh-epoch, unfenced mount body rather than leaving the old body in place. The mount write-fence deadline uses `CLOCK_BOOTTIME`, and expired-mount reclaim requires observing a stable holder token for a full `TTL + Drift` window on the reclaimer's own monotonic clock.", "issue_ids": ["rev.6"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/06-tla-models.md#cacasmountcore", "docs/superpowers/cas/how-we-got-here.md#turn-cursors-and-lease", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-13-cas-ref-lease-exclusivity-rev6.md#task-2", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-13-cas-ref-lease-exclusivity-rev6.md#task-2-amendment-2"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2622", "topic": "Mount reclaim must not trust the foreign mount body's", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B002-169"], "canonical_claim": "Mount reclaim must not trust the foreign mount body's wall-clock timestamp and must install the successor's own mount body.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/06-tla-models.md#cacasmountcore"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2623", "topic": "The per-write mount guard is a pure local check", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B002-170"], "canonical_claim": "The per-write mount guard is a pure local check `!lost \u2227 now < deadline`; superseded writers are blocked by the sticky local `lost` latch rather than a per-write shared-state read.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/06-tla-models.md#cacasmountcore"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2624", "topic": "The writer mount fence is the monotone writer_epoch stored", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B003-012"], "canonical_claim": "The writer mount fence is the monotone `writer_epoch` stored at `gc/server-roots/<srid>/epoch`, and a fencing failure is reported as a recoverable `MountFencedException`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/07-history.md#4-transition-table-phase-11-deliverable"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2625", "topic": "Each writer renewal uses one PUT carrying both the", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B003-033"], "canonical_claim": "Each writer renewal uses one `PUT` carrying both the mount lease and the build watermark `min_active`, plus one `gc/state` `GET` for the current round.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/07-s3-budget.md#write-budget-watermark"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2626", "topic": "The heartbeat floor phase costs one LIST of gc/server-roots/", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B003-046"], "canonical_claim": "The heartbeat floor phase costs one `LIST` of `gc/server-roots/`, N server-root `GET`s, and only rare token-guarded fence-out `PUT`s for expired mounts.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/07-s3-budget.md#gc-budget-floor"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2627", "topic": "Every durable write or delete captures the mount fence", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B004-025", "B023-104"], "canonical_claim": "Every durable write or delete captures the mount fence generation at admission and rechecks it immediately before the object-store call and on every conditional retry; reads are not fence-gated.; Every durable CAS PUT, DELETE, and staging finalize captures the fence generation at admission and throws typed error 668 before the backend call if mutation is no longer allowed or the generation changed; the check repeats before each conditional retry.", "issue_ids": ["C2", "D1"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#safety-invariants", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-22-cas-disk-lifecycle-rev7.md#L191-L215"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2628", "topic": "The local mount fence uses CLOCK_BOOTTIME, not CLOCK_MONOTONIC, so", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B004-051", "B004-152", "B005-037"], "canonical_claim": "The local mount fence uses `CLOCK_BOOTTIME`, not `CLOCK_MONOTONIC`, so a VM resumed from suspend observes the elapsed lease deadline.; The ack-floor redesign moves the mount-fence deadline to `CLOCK_BOOTTIME` because `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` does not advance during VM suspend. The local write-fence deadline should use a `CLOCK_BOOTTIME`-based clock because `steady_clock` (`CLOCK_MONOTONIC`) does not advance across VM suspend; container pause remains safe because the monotonic clock keeps ticking.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#mount-lease", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-02-cas-gc-ack-floor-fence.md#L869-L893", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-02-cas-gc-ack-floor-fence-redesign.md#safety-argument"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2629", "topic": "The mount lifecycle writes a terminal farewell with min_active", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B004-054", "B004-144"], "canonical_claim": "The mount lifecycle writes a terminal farewell with `min_active = UINT64_MAX` only after a certified drain; if the drain is not certified, no farewell is written.; `MountLeaseKeeper::terminate` stamps `expires_at_ms = now` and `min_active = UINT64_MAX`, merging the former watermark farewell into mount release.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#mount-lifecycle", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-02-cas-gc-ack-floor-fence.md#L502-L550"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2630", "topic": "A writer advertises observed_gc_round only after every in-flight mutateShard", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B004-140"], "canonical_claim": "A writer advertises `observed_gc_round` only after every in-flight `mutateShard` started under the older view has received its CAS response.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-02-cas-gc-ack-floor-fence.md#L18-L37"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2631", "topic": "Store::refreshViewForBeat loads gc/state and retired-list objects before swapping the", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B004-145"], "canonical_claim": "`Store::refreshViewForBeat` loads `gc/state` and retired-list objects before swapping the view under an exclusive `view_gate`; any read failure leaves the current round unchanged so the acknowledgement cannot advance.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-02-cas-gc-ack-floor-fence.md#L551-L614"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2632", "topic": "The writer beat holds a shared view_gate lock across", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B004-146"], "canonical_claim": "The writer beat holds a shared `view_gate` lock across the entire `mutateShard` operation, and `Store::open` loads the retire view before starting the keeper so the first heartbeat acknowledges the loaded round.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-02-cas-gc-ack-floor-fence.md#L551-L614"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2633", "topic": "If gc/state is unavailable during a writer heartbeat, the", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B005-015"], "canonical_claim": "If `gc/state` is unavailable during a writer heartbeat, the writer does not advance its GC acknowledgement but still renews its lease; this can stall deletion globally without taking writers down.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-02-cas-gc-ack-floor-fence-redesign.md#writer-protocol"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2634", "topic": "Writable open ordering is claim heartbeat, load gc/state and", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B005-016"], "canonical_claim": "Writable open ordering is claim heartbeat, load `gc/state` and the retired list, stamp `observed_gc_round`, and only then enable mutations.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-02-cas-gc-ack-floor-fence-redesign.md#writer-protocol"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2635", "topic": "A mount created during a GC pass either participates", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B005-036"], "canonical_claim": "A mount created during a GC pass either participates in the enumerated heartbeat floor or, if pagination misses it, loads a view at least as new as the last published round before its creation and therefore sees every entry eligible for graduation.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-02-cas-gc-ack-floor-fence-redesign.md#safety-argument"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2636", "topic": "A fence-out is terminal for an incarnation but a", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B005-038"], "canonical_claim": "A fence-out is terminal for an incarnation but a live server can self-remount by creating a fresh incarnation; `Store::tryRemountOnce` repeats the durable epoch bump, `claimMountAwaitingExpiry`, retired-view load, and write-fence arming, while older-epoch builds fail closed with `ABORTED`.", "issue_ids": ["S13"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-02-cas-gc-ack-floor-fence-redesign.md#self-remount-on-fence-out-liveness-counterpart-added-2026-07-02-after-the-first-soak"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2637", "topic": "claimMountAwaitingExpiry retries the unchanged claimMount only for a same-UUID", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B005-058"], "canonical_claim": "`claimMountAwaitingExpiry` retries the unchanged `claimMount` only for a same-UUID `LiveDoubleStart`; it immediately returns `ForeignOwner`, returns `Claimed` after token-guarded reclamation, and returns `LiveDoubleStart` after the bounded wait expires.", "issue_ids": ["S13"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-02-cas-mount-crash-recovery-design.md#wait-semantics"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2638", "topic": "Mount reclamation is safe because claimMount overwrites the observed", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B005-061"], "canonical_claim": "Mount reclamation is safe because `claimMount` overwrites the observed mount token conditionally; a live holder that renews between observation and reclaim changes the token and cannot be stolen.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-02-cas-mount-crash-recovery-design.md#why-the-wait-is-safe-token-guarded-not-timing-guarded"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2639", "topic": "Store::open calls claimMountAwaitingExpiry after bumping writer_epoch; it logs the", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B005-063"], "canonical_claim": "`Store::open` calls `claimMountAwaitingExpiry` after bumping `writer_epoch`; it logs the first wait and throws the existing `ABORTED` double-start error only for a foreign owner or a lease that remains live through the wait bound.", "issue_ids": ["S13"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-02-cas-mount-crash-recovery-design.md#storeopen-step-4--casstorecpp"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2640", "topic": "HeartbeatFloor gains max_ack, computed from every decoded mount body", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B005-091"], "canonical_claim": "`HeartbeatFloor` gains `max_ack`, computed from every decoded mount body including fenced and terminated bodies, because the rebuild needs the maximum observed acknowledgement to mint a safe round.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-03-cas-gc-rebuild.md#design-deltas-vs-the-spec-resolved-while-planning--the-spec-is-amended-by-this-section"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2641", "topic": "The mount-recovery plan requires a zero poll interval to", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B005-128"], "canonical_claim": "The mount-recovery plan requires a zero poll interval to be raised to a one-millisecond floor so the bounded wait cannot spin indefinitely.", "issue_ids": ["S13"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-02-cas-mount-crash-recovery.md#task-2-claimmountawaitingexpiry--bounded-wait-for-expiry-reclaim"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2642", "topic": "mayMutate is checked on every flush attempt; if the", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B006-010"], "canonical_claim": "`mayMutate` is checked on every flush attempt; if the fence trips, the whole batch fails with `ABORTED` and no caller receives a successful mutation.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-cas-shard-mutation-queue.md#L73-L74"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2643", "topic": "The queue leader holds the shared side of view_gate", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B006-013"], "canonical_claim": "The queue leader holds the shared side of `view_gate` for the entire flush, so a batch remains one in-flight mutation for the beat and drain protocol.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-cas-shard-mutation-queue.md#L80-L81"]}

